---
source: crates/surfaces/cli/src/main.rs
expression: command.clone().render_long_help().to_string()
---
Read the context a route of an extension declares for one entity: its sections fitted to a token budget in declared order, with a digest

Usage: specforge context [OPTIONS] <ROUTE> <SUBJECT>

Arguments:
  <ROUTE>
          The context route to read: its qualified name, `<extension>/<route>`

  <SUBJECT>
          The entity to read it for: one of the route's subject kind

Options:
      --max-tokens <MAX_TOKENS>
          Token budget for the pack (default: the route's own). The subject and the core sections are never cut, and a budget below them fails (E062); enrich sections are served whole in declared order, the first that does not fit keeps its most connected entities that fit, and the pack lists what was cut under `budget`

      --format <FORMAT>
          Format of the pack's graph

          Possible values:
          - context: every entity's fields, annotations, methods and verify, its headline on top, without file and line, for an agent
          - brief:   id, kind and title
          - graph:   every entity with its fields, file and line (Graph Protocol)
          
          [default: context]

      --record <ENTITY>
          Record the pack on ENTITY, a kind the route records whose subject is <SUBJECT>: the host writes the route, the pack's digest and its budget, with its ledger line, then prints the pack it recorded

      --path <PATH>
          A path inside the project (the nearest directory at or above it holding specforge.json; none: the directory itself, with the default configuration)
          
          [default: .]

      --pick <PATH>
          Print only the values at this path of the pack, one per line, e.g. '.digest' or '.budget.truncated_sections[]'. The path is steps: `.key`, `[n]` (digits, no leading zero), `[]` (every item) and `[<key>=<value>]` (the items whose field <key> is <value>, e.g. `[kind=plan]`: <key> may be dotted, `[fields.result=issue]`, and a number, boolean or null is compared as JSON, `[fields.backlog=true]`, `[fields.wave=2]`; an object or array is never selected); a `.` before `[` may be left out, and `.` alone is the whole answer. One line per value reached: a string bare (a newline in it is printed as is), an integer as is and any other number as an f64 (`1.0`, `-0.0`, `1e3` as `1000.0`), a boolean, object or array as compact JSON; null or a missing value prints nothing, and is not an error. Errors print as JSON, and the exit code is the command's.

      --count
          With --pick: print the number of values the path reaches, the lines --pick would print (null or a missing value is none, a string holding a newline is one), 0 for none; nothing when the command answered nothing, so `$(… --count) || n='?'` tells an unread answer from 0. It takes no --first, --last, --join or --fields (ADR 0325 D8).

      --first <N>
          With --pick: keep only the first N values it reaches

      --last <N>
          With --pick: keep only the last N values it reaches

      --join <SEP>
          With --pick: print the values kept on one line joined by SEP exactly as given (no escape is read: $'\n  ' is the shell's), then a newline; nothing when no value is reached. It combines with --first or --last, and takes no --count or --fields (ADR 0325 D8).

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')
